            <ArticleTitle>Sympathectomy induces novel purinergic sensitivity in sciatic afferents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1002-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To test the hypothesis that sympathectomy could induce novel purinergic sensitivity in sciatic afferents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Teased-fiber recordings were made from 32 spontaneously active A afferents from the sciatic nerves in surgically sympathectomized rats and 30 spontaneously active A afferents from the sciatic nerves in intact rats. Adenosine 5'-triphosphate (ATP) was injected via a cannula in jugular vein.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty eight percent of the spontaneously active afferent fibers from sciatic nerves in the sympathectomized rats responded to ATP, either with an increase or with a decrease in spontaneous firing. However, none of the fibers from the sciatic nerves in the intact rats was activated by ATP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sympathectomy induces novel purinergic sensitivity in A afferents from sciatic nerve.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
